D={
"Battle of Badr":"In March 624 about 313 Muslims from Medina intercepted a Meccan caravan and fought a Quraysh army roughly three times larger at the wells of Badr. The Muslim victory, seen as divine support (the Quran refers to angels), killed several Meccan leaders including Abu Jahl. It established Muhammad as a political and military leader and strengthened the community in Medina.",
"Battle of Qadisiyyah":"In 636 an Arab army under Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas met the Sasanian Persian army led by Rustam near Kufa in Iraq. After three or four days of fighting, including against Persian war elephants, Rustam was killed. The victory opened Mesopotamia, and the capital Ctesiphon fell in 637; Persia gradually converted to Islam while keeping its language and culture.",
"Xuanzang's journey to India":"Defying a travel ban, the monk Xuanzang left Chang'an in 629 and crossed the Taklamakan desert and the Pamirs to reach India. He studied for years at Nalanda under Shilabhadra, debated at Harsha's court, and returned in 645 with 657 texts, which he spent his life translating. His 'Great Tang Records on the Western Regions' describes Indian cities, customs and rulers and guided archaeologists to sites like Nalanda.",
"Tang–Tibet wars":"Under Songtsen Gampo, Tibet became an empire in the 7th century; he married a Chinese princess (Wencheng) and a Nepali princess, bringing Buddhism. Tibet and Tang China fought for the Tarim basin's Silk Road oasis cities for two centuries. In 763, when the Tang was weakened by the An Lushan rebellion, Tibetan armies briefly occupied Chang'an; a treaty pillar of 821–822 in Lhasa records peace.",
"An Lushan rebellion":"An Lushan, a general of Sogdian–Turkic descent who commanded border armies, rebelled in 755, capturing Luoyang and Chang'an. Emperor Xuanzong fled, and his beloved consort Yang Guifei was executed by his own troops. The war lasted until 763 with help from Uyghur mercenaries; census numbers dropped sharply, and the Tang never fully recovered central control.",
"Charlemagne crowned emperor":"Charlemagne, king of the Franks, had conquered the Lombards and Saxons and protected Pope Leo III from Roman enemies. On Christmas Day 800, Leo crowned him 'Emperor of the Romans' in St Peter's. The title, rejected by Byzantium, laid the basis for the Holy Roman Empire and long rivalry between popes and emperors.",
"Viking raid on Lindisfarne":"On 8 June 793 Norse raiders attacked the island monastery of Lindisfarne off Northumberland, killing monks and seizing treasures. The scholar Alcuin wrote that such terror had never appeared in Britain. It marks the start of the Viking Age, when Scandinavians raided, traded and settled from Ireland to Russia.",
"Battle of Manzikert":"On 26 August 1071 the Seljuk sultan Alp Arslan defeated the Byzantine army in eastern Anatolia and captured Emperor Romanos IV Diogenes, after some Byzantine units deserted. Civil war at Constantinople followed, and Turkish groups poured into Anatolia, which became 'Turkey'. The Byzantine plea for help led to the First Crusade.",
"Mahmud of Ghazni raids Somnath":"Mahmud, ruler of Ghazni in Afghanistan, raided north India about 17 times between 1000 and 1027, looting temple wealth from Mathura, Kannauj and Thanesar. In 1025–26 he plundered the Somnath temple in Gujarat. Al-Biruni accompanied his court and wrote a study of India; the temple was rebuilt many times, most recently in 1951.",
"Angkor Wat built":"Suryavarman II built Angkor Wat between about 1113 and 1150 as a temple to Vishnu and probably his own mausoleum, facing west. Its five towers represent Mount Meru, and 800 metres of bas-reliefs show the churning of the ocean of milk and battles from the Mahabharata and Ramayana. It later became a Buddhist site and is now on Cambodia's flag.",
"Saladin retakes Jerusalem":"Saladin united Egypt and Syria and, on 4 July 1187, destroyed the crusader army at the Horns of Hattin. He took Jerusalem in October after negotiations, allowing many Christians to ransom themselves, in contrast to the 1099 massacre. The Third Crusade under Richard the Lionheart failed to retake it.",
"Fourth Crusade sacks Constantinople":"Crusaders, indebted to Venice for ships, were diverted to Constantinople to back a Byzantine claimant. In April 1204 they stormed and sacked the city, looting churches and taking the bronze horses now in Venice. A Latin Empire ruled until 1261, permanently weakening Byzantium and the relationship between Catholic and Orthodox churches.",
"Battle of Ain Jalut":"On 3 September 1260 the Mamluks of Egypt under Qutuz and Baybars defeated a Mongol army in Galilee, the first major check on Mongol expansion. Hulagu had withdrawn much of his army after the Great Khan's death. Baybars soon killed Qutuz and became sultan.",
"Marco Polo in China":"The Venetian Marco Polo travelled with his father and uncle along the Silk Road, reaching Kublai Khan around 1275 and serving him for 17 years. Imprisoned in Genoa after returning, he dictated his travels to Rustichello. His book described paper money, coal and great cities, and inspired Columbus.",
"Mongol invasions of Japan":"Kublai Khan demanded Japan's submission; when refused, he sent a fleet in 1274 and a much larger one, possibly 140,000 men, in 1281. Samurai fought fiercely and built a stone wall at Hakata Bay, and typhoons — the kamikaze or 'divine winds' — destroyed the fleets. The failure strained the Kamakura shogunate.",
"Great Famine of Europe":"Torrential rains from 1315 to 1317 rotted crops across northern Europe, followed by livestock disease. Perhaps 5–12% of the population died, and there were reports of cannibalism. It marked the end of medieval population growth, just before the Black Death.",
"Hundred Years' War":"English kings claimed the French throne; the war (1337–1453) saw English longbow victories at Crécy, Poitiers and Agincourt. Joan of Arc helped lift the siege of Orléans (1429), and France eventually drove the English out except from Calais. The war strengthened national identities and the use of cannon.",
"Timur sacks Delhi":"Timur invaded India in 1398, defeated Sultan Nasir-ud-din Mahmud Tughlaq, and sacked Delhi in December. He massacred about 100,000 prisoners before the battle and took artisans to build Samarkand. The Tughlaq state collapsed; his descendant Babur founded the Mughal Empire.",
"Zheng He's voyages":"Between 1405 and 1433, the Muslim eunuch admiral Zheng He led seven Ming fleets of up to 300 ships and 27,000 men to Southeast Asia, Calicut, Hormuz, Arabia and East Africa. They brought back tributes, including a giraffe from Malindi. After 1433 the Ming stopped the voyages and turned inward.",
"Battle of Grunwald":"On 15 July 1410 the combined army of Poland and Lithuania under Władysław II Jagiełło and Vytautas defeated the Teutonic Knights, killing their Grand Master. It was one of the largest battles of medieval Europe and broke the Order's power.",
"Fall of Granada and Reconquista":"The Christian reconquest of Iberia from Muslim rulers took over 700 years. Ferdinand and Isabella besieged Granada, the last Nasrid kingdom, which surrendered on 2 January 1492. That year Jews were expelled, Muslims were later forced to convert, and Columbus sailed under the same monarchs.",
"Portuguese take Goa":"Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate in 1510, with help from local ally Timmayya. Goa became the capital of Portugal's Asian empire and a centre of Catholic missions, including the Inquisition (1560). It remained Portuguese until India took it in 1961.",
"Protestant Reformation":"Martin Luther, a German monk, attacked the sale of indulgences in his Ninety-five Theses (1517), arguing salvation came through faith alone. Printing spread his ideas; Zwingli, Calvin and Henry VIII's break with Rome followed. Wars of religion and the Catholic Counter-Reformation reshaped Europe.",
"Battle of Khanwa":"In March 1527 Babur faced a confederacy of Rajputs and Afghans led by Rana Sanga of Mewar. Babur declared a jihad, renounced wine, and won using guns and the tulughma. The victory secured Mughal rule in north India.",
"Second Battle of Panipat":"After Humayun's death, Hemu, a Hindu general of the Suri dynasty, took Delhi and proclaimed himself Raja Vikramaditya. On 5 November 1556 Akbar's army under Bairam Khan fought him; an arrow struck Hemu's eye, his army broke, and he was executed. Akbar's long reign began.",
"Battle of Haldighati":"On 18 June 1576 Maharana Pratap of Mewar fought Akbar's army led by Man Singh of Amber. Pratap's horse Chetak was wounded carrying him to safety. Though the Mughals held the field, Pratap continued guerrilla resistance and recovered much of Mewar.",
"Spanish Armada defeated":"In 1588 Philip II sent 130 ships to invade England. English fireships and faster ships scattered it at Gravelines, and storms wrecked many ships off Scotland and Ireland. England's victory boosted Protestantism and naval ambitions.",
"Imjin War":"In 1592 Toyotomi Hideyoshi invaded Korea with 158,000 troops, intending to conquer China. Admiral Yi Sun-sin's turtle ships cut Japanese supply lines, Ming China intervened, and Hideyoshi's death in 1598 ended the war, leaving Korea devastated.",
"Battle of Sekigahara":"On 21 October 1600 Tokugawa Ieyasu's Eastern Army defeated the Western Army, helped by defections. It ended the Sengoku period; Ieyasu became shogun in 1603 and his family ruled Japan for 265 years.",
"Dutch East India Company founded":"Founded in 1602, the VOC was the first company to issue public shares on a stock exchange (Amsterdam). With power to wage war and make treaties, it seized the Spice Islands, based itself in Batavia (Jakarta), and controlled the nutmeg and clove trade through violence, including the massacre of Banda islanders (1621).",
"Jamestown founded":"In 1607 the Virginia Company founded Jamestown, the first permanent English colony in America. Many settlers starved; tobacco became the cash crop, and in 1619 the first enslaved Africans arrived in Virginia. Relations with the Powhatan Confederacy included Pocahontas's marriage to John Rolfe.",
"English Civil War":"Conflicts between Charles I and Parliament over taxes and religion led to war (1642–51). Oliver Cromwell's New Model Army won; Charles I was executed in 1649 and England became a republic. The monarchy was restored in 1660.",
"Manchu conquest of China":"The Manchus, united by Nurhaci, entered China in 1644 after rebels took Beijing and a Ming general opened the Great Wall's gate. They ordered Han men to wear the queue hairstyle and conquered the south over decades, founding the Qing dynasty.",
"Shivaji's coronation":"On 6 June 1674 at Raigad, Shivaji was crowned Chhatrapati with Vedic rites performed by Gaga Bhatt, proclaiming an independent Maratha state. He started a new era and issued coins.",
"Glorious Revolution":"In 1688 Parliament invited William of Orange to replace the Catholic James II, who fled. The Bill of Rights (1689) limited royal power and established parliamentary supremacy.",
"Death of Aurangzeb":"Aurangzeb died in 1707 after 25 years of war in the Deccan. His successors were weak; nawabs became independent and the Marathas expanded, beginning Mughal decline.",
"Nadir Shah sacks Delhi":"In 1739 Persia's Nadir Shah defeated the Mughals at Karnal and sacked Delhi, killing about 20,000–30,000 and carrying off the Peacock Throne and the Koh-i-Noor.",
}
